Design Memo

A tree is appropriate for representing the doctor structure because
it organizes doctors into parent-child relationships. Each doctor
can be represented by a node containing their name and pointers to
their left and right reports. This structure makes it easier to
represent reporting relationships and recursively explore different
parts of the hierarchy. However, a binary tree only permits two
direct reports per doctor, so a general tree would be more suitable
for a real hospital where doctors may supervise many people.

Software engineers use preorder, inorder, and postorder traversals
for different purposes. Preorder visits the parent before its
children, making it useful for copying a tree or displaying a
hierarchy from top to bottom. Inorder visits the left subtree,
the parent, and then the right subtree. It is especially useful
for retrieving values in sorted order from a binary search tree.
Postorder visits both subtrees before the parent, making it useful
for deleting a tree or processing child elements before their
parent. Each traversal visits every node once, so its time
complexity is O(n), where n is the number of nodes.

Heaps help simulate real-time systems such as emergency intake
because they keep the highest-priority patient at the root.
When a new patient arrives, the heap efficiently inserts them
according to urgency. When staff are ready to treat the next
patient, the highest-priority patient can be removed without
sorting the entire queue. In a binary min-heap, insertion and
removal take O(log n) time, while checking the next patient
takes O(1) time. This allows a busy system to manage changing
priorities efficiently.
